an asylum seeker who was told he would  be going on this flight to rwanda has  given us his account of what happened  we've been following the progress of an  iraqi man zahir not his real name who's  spoken to us by phone from kolnbrook  detention center near heathrow talk me  through  what happened yesterday they say that  you have  come to take to the plate  i see my face  i see that the court hasn't finished it  yet they say you have to take you and i  i don't have so  they take me by four they  it was the four of them two of them take  my hand um  take my  feet and one of them  so what happened after that so then were  you put in a van  push me  out of seven men taken to the airport  five boarded the plane tuesday night but  at the last minute all had their  deportation at the very least postponed  after the european court of human rights  ruled in one man's appeal that the  government needed to wait for next  month's judicial review which will rule  on whether the rwanda policy itself is  legal the courts in london decided that  it was fine for them to be sent to  rwanda on the basis they would be there  for only a few weeks before the high  court reaches a decision by the end of  next month the strasbourg court the  human rights court decided that they  couldn't be sure they would be brought  back and therefore took a different view  sophie lucas whose firm represents three  of the men says this shouldn't be  portrayed as the eu overruling the uk  the european convention on human rights  was part drafted and signed by winston  churchill and is incorporated into our  uk domestic laws  the question here is not we broke the  rules how can we get rid of them it's we  broke the rules how can we make policies  that comply with them the home secretary  says the policy is legal and humane  it has saddened me madam deputy speaker  to see rwanda so terribly misrepresented  introduced in his century  it is another example of how all too  often critics not only don't know what  they're speaking about but seek to  vilify another international country  that has a good track record when it  comes to refugees and stepping up to  international responsibilities shadow  home secretary yvette cooper the  opposition says the policy was always  set to fail this is not and never has  been a serious policy and she knew that  when she chartered the plane  she knew that among the people she was  planning to send to rwanda on this plane  were torture and trafficking victims the  government insists they need to stop the  cruel trafficking of asylum seekers  across the channel and more than a  hundred more arrive today  but zahir who's made this crossing says  actually the government is behaving just  like the traffickers i told them i told  them  that you and the trafficker  yeah  told us to  do something and we say not and  so you were reminded of what it was like  being trafficked  yeah it was the same thing it was not  different this is that uk  democratic country they say where is the  human right i don't know  just tell you the difference between  them and the traffickers  the security firm involved mighty told  sky news our focus is on treating the  people in our care with dignity and  respect and we are confident that our  officers have acted professionally the  firm said staff wore body cameras and  restraint is only ever used as a last  resort to ensure the safety of those  traveling and their staff members jason  farrell sky news
 

